From: Waltteri Hooli <1420194+wahooli@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] local: clk: qcom: gcc-x1e80100: keep the display AXI clock through the unused sweep

The intermittent blue screen on the Yoga Slim 7x is the DMA0 display pipe
never fetching a pixel after msm's first modeset. The interface underruns five
lines into active video, the MDP answers with a hardware-initiated CTL reset,
and that reset never completes. Measured live on a blue boot (2026-10-05):

  - the SMMU context for the display is clean: translating, no fault, no stall;
  - forcing the reset through with CTL_SW_RESET_OVERRIDE works, and re-sending
    the lost flush is consumed at the next vsync, yet DMA0's fetch counters
    stay at 0 and the underrun and reset come straight back;
  - DMA0 is VBIF client 1, and its idle bit in VBIF_XIN_HALT_CTRL1 is clear in
    every failing capture, even after 30 minutes without a single fetch;
  - asked to halt, the idle VIG0 client acks in 1 us, DMA0's client never acks
    in 200 ms. A client cannot halt while one of its reads is outstanding.

So DMA0 has a memory read outstanding that will never retire. Nothing in the
MDSS clears it: not the CTL reset, not the MDSS core reset at probe. Only a
power cycle does.

The only point in boot where Linux cuts the display's memory path under a live
transfer is clk_disable_unused(), at 2.0 s. GCC is built in, so
gcc_disp_hf_axi_clk exists then, has no consumer yet (msm loads at ~4.3 s), and
the firmware's boot display is actively fetching through it. Gating it strands
whatever read is in flight. Then msm_mdss_reset() asserts the MDSS core reset
with the clock still off, and the port on the far side of the AXI interface
is left holding a transaction the reset master no longer knows about. Whether
a read is in flight at that instant is down to timing, which fits a failure
that hits roughly one boot in ten.

Glymur, the next generation of this SoC, marks the same clock CLK_IS_CRITICAL.
Take the narrower fix here: CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED keeps it running only until msm
owns it, after which runtime PM gates it normally while the display is idle.

Expected: no blue boots over enough warm reboots to matter (baseline 4 in 39),
and the firmware console should no longer freeze or blank at ~2 s before msm
takes over.

--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-x1e80100.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-x1e80100.c
@@ -2999,6 +2999,15 @@
 		.hw.init = &(const struct clk_init_data) {
 			.name = "gcc_disp_hf_axi_clk",
 			.ops = &clk_branch2_ops,
+			/*
+			 * The firmware's boot display is still fetching through
+			 * this clock when clk_disable_unused() runs, seconds
+			 * before msm loads. Gating it under a live scanout can
+			 * strand an AXI read that the MDSS reset at msm probe
+			 * cannot reach, and that pipe never fetches again.
+			 * msm takes ownership of the clock once it probes.
+			 */
+			.flags = CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED,
 		},
 	},
 };
